{
  "image_name": "1d4c97ccd66d7ccd6112287a76bfb4d3.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure illustrates why immune control of metastasis can fail as tumour cells spread from a primary tumour to lymph nodes and distant organs. On the left, a primary tumour contains tumour-specific CD8+ T cells, while some cancer cells acquire metastatic traits and enter the blood or lymphatic vessels. The central vessel emphasizes that both metastatic cells and T cells circulate, but their fates diverge.\n\nThe main message is that metastasis creates several immune obstacles. First, during evolution of the primary tumour, the tumour-specific CD8+ T-cell population may become functionally exhausted, leaving it less capable of controlling metastatic outgrowth. Second, when malignant cells invade the tumour-draining lymph node, they may disrupt an important local source of functional tumour-specific T cells. Third, once tumour cells seed organs such as lung, liver, or brain, the available T-cell repertoire appears both reduced in number and quality because of prior engagement in the primary tumour.\n\nThe figure also highlights practical barriers in distant tissues: circulating CD8+ T cells must locate and infiltrate metastatic lesions, and then adapt to very different tissue environments. Overall, the image presents metastasis as both a dispersal process for cancer cells and a progressive weakening of antitumour T-cell surveillance.",
